240ES c  $ Two studies have claimed first observation of {+244}Md, identified   
240ES2c  via |a decay: one from GSI (2020Kh08) and the other from LBNL          
240ES3c  (2020Po07 - the evaluator acknowledges being a coauthor on this        
240ES4c  publication). The LBNL study used the {+209}Bi({+40}Ar,2n) reaction    
240ES5c  (E{-beam}=220 MeV before entering the 500 |mg/cm{+2} targets) while    
240ES6c  the GSI experiment employed the {+197}Au({+50}Ti,xn) reaction at       
240ES7c  231.5 and 239.8 MeV (center of target), thereby observing both the 2n  
240ES8c  and 3n channels producing {+245}Md and {+244}Md, respectively.  Both   
240ES9c  experiments identified reactions of interest using genetic             
240ESac  correlations, whereby a fusion-evaporation residue is separated from   
240ESbc  unreacted beam and implanted in a position-sensitive focal-plane       
240EScc  detector, and the energies and decay times of subsequent decays        
240ESdc  occuring at the same location are correlated with the implanted ions.  
240ESec  Additionally, the LBNL experiment could retract the focal plane        
240ESfc  detector to send reaction products through a gas catcher and           
240ESgc  secondary separator in order to perform direct mass measurements of    
240EShc  recoil products. However, the two studies are in disagreement.  The    
240ESic  GSI data at 231.5 MeV (E{-|a}=8.61-8.69 MeV, T{-1/2}=0.33 s {I+15-8})  
240ESjc  is consistent with the decay properties of {+245}Md, while the data    
240ESkc  at 239.8 MeV is claimed to be the first observation of |a decay of     
240ESlc  {+244}Md (E{-|a}=8.73-8.86 MeV, T{-1/2}=0.30 s {I+19-9}).  The LBNL    
240ESmc  data reports an |a-decay energy of 8.66 MeV {I2} with a half-life of   
240ESnc  0.4 s {I+4-1}, and possibly a second decay mode with E{-|a}=8.31 MeV   
240ESoc  {I2} and T{-1/2}|?6 s.  The main decay mode observed in the LBNL       
240ESpc  experiment is consistent with the energy and half-life reported for    
240ESqc  {+245}Md in the GSI experiment, which might suggest that the LBNL      
240ESrc  result is a misidentification.  However, the direct mass measurement   
240ESsc  employed at LBNL makes it difficult to dismiss the results as an       
240EStc  error.  Unfortunately, supporting spectroscopic data from a            
240ESuc  simultaneous measurement of {+247}Md during the LBNL experiment have   
240ESvc  not been published, which would help to corroborate the {+244}Md       
240ESwc  data. Given the experimental ambiguity, the evaluator has opted not    
240ESxc  to provide evaluated values until further evidence is available.       
